- ✅ Refactored all backend functions to use dynamic loops (functions_meter.ino, functions_api.ino, functions_web.ino)
- ✅ Updated JavaScript to use loops instead of hardcoded if-else chains
- ✅ Maintained 100% backward compatibility with existing 4-meter systems
- ✅ **Tested and verified**: Compiles and runs successfully on ESP32
- ✅ **Backend fully scalable**: Only HTML templates need expansion for 10+ meters

#### Session 3: UI Redesign and Meters Page (October 2024)
- ✅ Redesigned web interface with modern sidebar layout using flexbox
- ✅ Added Energy Consumption Summary table (L1, L2, L3, Total)
- ✅ Added Power Consumption Summary table (L1, L2, L3, Total)
- ✅ Created dedicated Meters page (/meters?id=N) for single-meter details
- ✅ Removed individual meter sections from home page (cleaner interface)
- ✅ Fixed CSS issues: className → class, removed conflicting span rule
- ✅ Implemented handleMeters() with query string parsing
- ✅ **Page Structure**: Home (summaries), Meters (details), Settings, Admin

#### Session 4: 5-Meter Expansion and WebSocket Fix (October 2024)
- ✅ Expanded from 4 to 5 meters: Updated `maxNumberOfMeters = 5` in open_energy_gateway.ino
- ✅ Added meter 5 HTML sections to web_home.h (meter list, energy summary, power summary)
- ✅ Added meter 5 HTML section to web_settings.h
- ✅ Fixed bug: Corrected row IDs for meters 3,4,5 in power summary table (m3_active_power, m4_active_power, m5_active_power)
- ✅ Updated JavaScript: Changed `maxNumberOfMeters = 5` in web_home.h
- ✅ **Critical WebSocket Fix**: Added missing `webSocketEvent()` handler in functions_web.ino
- ✅ **String Mutation Fix**: Changed handleRoot() to use local copy `webpage_home_local` instead of mutating global
- ✅ Added WebSocket error handling: WStype_ERROR case and default case
- ✅ Fixed typo: Line 247 `svoid` → `void initNTP()`
- ✅ **WebSocket Performance Fix**: Added `webSocket.loop()` and `server.handleClient()` inside meter reading loop
- ✅ **Retry Optimization**: Reduced WebSocket reconnect delay from 5 seconds to 2 seconds in all web files
- ✅ **Uploaded and Tested**: ESP32 successfully running with all 5 meters operational
- ✅ **Performance Verified**: Connection time reduced from 80 seconds to 12 seconds (85% improvement)
- ✅ **Testing with Playwright MCP**: Confirmed all 5 meters displaying real-time data correctly

**Key Learnings**: 
- WebSocket requires `webSocket.onEvent(webSocketEvent)` BEFORE `webSocket.begin()` or connections fail with ERR_CONNECTION_RESET
- Blocking Modbus reads (5 meters × 1-2 seconds) prevented WebSocket handshakes from completing
- Solution: Call `webSocket.loop()` and `server.handleClient()` between each meter read for responsive connections

#### Session 5: Staggered Meter Reading Optimization (January 2025)
- ✅ Implemented sequential meter reading: ONE meter per second instead of batch processing all 5
- ✅ Added `currentMeterIndex` global variable for meter cycling (1→2→3→4→5→1...)
- ✅ Changed `METER_CONNECTION_INTERVAL` from 5000ms to 1000ms
- ✅ Modified main loop (lines 353-369) to read single meter per iteration
- ✅ WebSocket broadcast occurs immediately after each meter read (progressive updates)
- ✅ **Performance Achievement**: Reduced connection time from 13 seconds to <3 seconds (77% improvement)
- ✅ **User Experience**: First meter data appears in 1-2 seconds, not 13 seconds
- ✅ Each meter still refreshes every 5 seconds (5 meters × 1 second = 5-second cycle)
- ✅ Added Gateway Reboot functionality with 15-second countdown and auto-redirect
- ✅ Updated web interface message: "10 seconds for connection" → "2-3 seconds"
- ✅ **Production tested**: Validated on ESP32 hardware with all 5 meters

**Key Learnings**:
- Batch reading all meters creates worst-case wait time for WebSocket handshake
- Sequential reading with immediate broadcast provides much better perceived performance
- 1-second intervals allow responsive WebSocket servicing while maintaining data freshness
- Progressive data population superior UX compared to delayed batch updates

